  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` and wrote tests for it in a new file, `tests/test_money.py`. The full suite passes, all 11 tests, using `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`. Nothing is committed.\n\n```python\nif parts < 1:\n    raise ValueError(f\"parts must be at least 1: {parts}\")\nbase, extra = divmod(cents, parts)\nreturn [base + 1 if i < extra else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\nSo `split(10, 3)` gives `[4, 3, 3]` and `split(11, 4)` gives `[3, 3, 3, 2]`. The tests cover:\n- an even split\n- both of those remainder cases\n- `parts=1`\n- `cents=0`\n- `parts=0` and `parts=-2`, which both raise `ValueError`\n\n**Decision for you:** negative amounts don't follow the \"extra cent goes first\" rule. `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`: the total is still -10, but the largest share in absolute terms comes last. If you want it to mirror the positive case (`[-4, -3, -3]`), the fix is to split the absolute value and negate the result. Tell me which you want, or whether negative amounts should raise an error instead.",
